<Layout title="Research — reetom" description="Bayesian models for sequential data, exploratory studies of deep learning, and reproducible scientific computing."><Intro title="Research"><p>I'm interested in understanding how machine learning models learn, what assumptions shape their behavior, and how we can make them more reliable. My work spans Bayesian nonparametric methods for discovering latent structure, the optimization dynamics of deep neural networks, and emerging applications of autonomous AI agents. I'm particularly drawn to problems involving sequential data, uncertainty, and complex learning systems.</p></Intro>
<div class="research-body">
<section id="sequential" class="entry"><h2>Bayesian nonparametrics and sequential data</h2><p>In my honors thesis at Boston University, I studied how to incorporate temporal structure into Bayesian clustering to identify interpretable market regimes in financial time series. Professor Luis Carvalho advised the project.</p><p>I compared time-dependent and sticky Dirichlet process mixture models with hidden Markov models. In retrospective inference, later observations can help clarify an earlier transition. I explored how this future-aware modeling affects the regimes we identify.</p><a href="/projects/#regime-identification">Read about the thesis →</a></section>
<section id="optimization" class="entry"><h2>Deep learning and optimization</h2><p>In the ongoing Temporal EOS project, I'm exploring whether temporal dependence in training data affects neural network optimization near the edge of stability.</p><p>I use controlled synthetic experiments to track Hessian sharpness, loss oscillations, and generalization while varying how observations are sampled. These comparisons help me investigate the roles of input conditioning and dependence between examples.</p><a href="/projects/#temporal-eos">Explore Temporal EOS →</a></section>
<section id="computing" class="entry"><h2>Scientific computing and research software</h2><p>At Boston University Research Computing Services, I build scientific workflow tools that help researchers reproduce computational experiments on HPC systems. My work on defcon captures the dependencies of batch jobs in editable container definitions.</p><p>With Hawkeye, I'm exploring autonomous performance analysis. The agent uses Bayesian optimization and active system identification to choose experiments that reveal how resources interact.</p><a href="/projects/#hawkeye">Explore Hawkeye →</a></section>
<section class="entry"><h2>Research interests</h2><p>Deep learning · Statistical machine learning · Bayesian nonparametrics · Probabilistic modeling · Uncertainty quantification · Optimization dynamics · Agentic AI and autonomous systems · Time series and sequential modeling · Causal inference · Scientific computing · Bioinformatics</p></section></div></Layout>
